The Macedonians vote in a referendum on changing the country's name
The people of Macedonia on Sunday voted in a referendum on whether to change the country's name to the "Northern Commendary Republika", taking a step that will prevent decades-long controversy with Greece, which has blocked Macedonia's requests for accession to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, emphasizes that the name of its northern neighbour testifies to claims to its territory and has vetoped Macedonia's accession to NATO and the EU.
Both governments entered into an agreement in June, which was based on the proposed new name, but nationalists in the opposition are insisting that the changes would undermine the Ethnic identity of the militia militia.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said that I will not vote in a referendum, and a campaign calling for a boycott has argued whether voter turnout will reach at least 50%, which is necessary for the referendum to be declared valid.
A question is asked in the referendum: “Whether you’re in favour of joining NATO and the EU by concluding an agreement with Greece.”
Proponents of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that this is the price worth paying on the way to Macedonia, one of the countries that arose after the break-up of Yugoslavia to become an organization, such as an EU-NATO member.
"I have come to vote for the future of the country, for the youth of Macedonia, so that they can live freely, under the guise of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," said 79-year-old Oliver Georgijevsky (Oliver Georgijevsky).
Although it is not legally binding, sufficient members of parliament have stated that it will take into account the outcome of the vote, which will become decisive.
In order to change the name, a 2/3 majority will be needed in parliament.
The National Electoral Commission informed that there have been reports of misconflicted violations until one in the afternoon.
However, the turnout was only 16 per cent, compared to 34 per cent in the previous parliamentary elections, in which 66 per per cent of registered voters voted.
"I came to vote for my children, our place in Europe," said 62-year-old Josie Tanevsky (Gjose Tanevski), a voter in Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, his wife Zorica and their son Dushko cast their votes in a referendum in Macedonia for changing the name of the country, which will open the way to NATO and the European Union, was handed over to Strumica (Strumica) on September 30, 2018.
The 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarakov (Vladimir Kavardarkov) prepared a small stage in front of Skopier and arranged chairs near the tents that had been placed by those who would boycott the referendum.
"We're in favour of NATO and the EU, but we want to arrive with a raised head, not through a servant door," Cavadrokov said.
"We're a small country, but we have self-reliance.
If they don't want to accept us like Macedonia, we can appeal to others, such as China or Russia, and become a part of Europe-Asian integration."
Prime Minister Zayev argues that participation in NATO will start the necessary investments in Macedonia, where the unemployment rate exceeds 20 per cent.
"I believe that the vast majority will pass the vote, because more than 80" percent of our citizens support the EU and NATO," Zayev said after conveying the vote.
He said the result “yes” “confirms our future”.
The Macedonia's Institute for the Policy Research survey published last Monday shows a 30-43 per per cent of voters, less than the activity they need, taking part in the referendum.
Another poll conducted by the Macedonian “Telma TV” found that 57 percent of voters were planning to take part in Sunday's vote.
70 per cent of them replied that they would vote in favour.
In order for the referendum to be successful, voter turnout needs to be 50 per cent and another vote.
Failure of the referendum will be the first serious blow to the West-oriented government, which came to power last May.
