Morality's soldier rhetoric has streaked the rest of the goodwill in the Chileans, she said.
Bolivia and Chile will once continue negotiations, but after this it will be very difficult to maintain a discussion.
Both countries have not exchanged envoy since 1962.
Bolivia's spokeswoman in The Hague, former president Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, did not agree that the court made the decision unusually quickly.
On Monday, Bolivia will have an "extreme opportunity to start a new era in a relationship with Chile" and the opportunity to "to put an end to 139-year-long conflicts from which both sides will benefit," he said.
Kalzadilla also denigrated that Morales, still one of Latin America's most popular presidents, used maritime problems as political crutches.
"Bolivia will never relinquish its right of access to the Quiet Ocean," he added.
"This decision is an opportunity to realise what needs to be done to overcome the past."
