Jones Edvards: knowledge of languages is essential for citizens of the world
Scottish independent schools have a reputation for outstanding educational institutions, and it has retained it in 2018, when the results of outstanding exams were obtained once again, which are further reinforced by individual and collective achievements in sports, art in music and other social events.
The aim of these schools with a total of 30 000 learners in Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) is to ensure the best level for their learners and their parents.
Independent schools have set the goal of preparing their learners for further and tertiary education, their chosen quarry and their lives as citizens of the world.
As an education sector that can develop and implement special school curriculum, we see that modern languages are still a popular and desirable subject in our schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you talk to a person the language he understands, it remains in his consciousness.
If you speak his mother tongue, it remains in his heart.”
It's convincing a reminder that we can't just rely on the English language when we want to build a relationship and gain the trust of people from other countries.
Taking into account the recent results of this year's exams, we can see that the languages are at the top of the league's tables with the largest number of exams in independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of students who taught foreign languages reached the highest grade A.
Scottish Indifferent Schools Council data for 74 schools shows that 72 per cent of pupils earned the highest mark in A Chinese, and the same mark was also scored by 72 per cent who learned German, 69 per cent, who mastered French and 63 per cent who mastered Spanish.
This demonstrates the support of an Independent School in Sotley as vital skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages as the subject-matter of choice are now judged in independent school curriculum as the subjects of the science-engine-engineering and mathematics (science, technology, engineering and mathematics).
A 2014 survey of the British Employment and Skills Commission (Commission for Employment and Sequences) revealed that 17 per per Employers cited a lack of language proficiencies as a reason for incapacitated vacancies.
Therefore, more and more often language proficial skills become mandatory in order to prepare young people for their careers in the future.
Increasing job opportunities, which will require language proficiency, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Irrespective of the chosen career, by learning a foreign language, they will in the future gain a true advantage provided by such long-term skills.
Ability to communicate directly directly with people from other countries will automatically improve the competitiveness of a multi-lingual connoisseur.
According to YouGov's 2013 survey involving more than 4,000 adults in Great Britain, 75 per cent didn't know a foreign language well enough to maintain a conversation, but the French language was the only one in which a two-digit number-appropriate was spoken, 15 per cent.
That's why investing in language learning is important for today's children.
A number of languages, in particular language proficiencies in developing countries, will provide children with a more meaningful job.
School in Scottish differs in terms of taught languages.
Some schools will pay more attention to the most customary modern languages, while others will teach languages, such as Chinese and Japanese, which are considered the most paramount for Britain for the future, according to plans by 2020.
Whatever your child will be of interest, you will always be able to choose from a number of languages in independent schools, and teachers will be specialists in the relevant field.
Scottish Independent schools are determined to provide a linguistic environment that prepares the children and will provide them with the skills they need to succeed, whatever the future.
At the moment, in the global business environment, it can not be denied that languages are still vital for the future of the country, so this fact must be reflected in education.
Indeed, modern languages should be regarded as "inter-companied communication skills".
Independent schools will continue to offer Skotija's young people this choice, diversity and excellence.
Il faut bien le faire (That has to be done).
John Edvards is the director of Scots' Independent Schools Council
