"We need to do that now," he said of a start-to-company.
“That is a historic moment.”
The app uses "Solid" technology so that people can create their own "personal online data repository,"
It can include contact lists, to-do lists, calendar, music library, as well as other personal and professional tools.
It's the same as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify would be all simultaneously available in one browser.
A personal online data repository is unique in that only the user determines who and what type of information can be accessed.
An enterprise calls it “personal powers using data”.
According John Bruce, CEO John Bruce's idea is to provide the company's resources, processes and appropriate skills to make "Solid" accessible to everyone.
The company currently operates Burner-Lee, Bruce, an IBM-builted security platform, some freelance developers hired to work on the project, and a community of voluntary encoders.
Starting this week, tech developers around the world could create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
