“National Trust” eavesdropping the life of secret bats
The new study, being carried out under the property of the Scottish Highland, is aimed at discovering how bats use the landscape in the process of searching.
Detecostions are expected to bring new clarity to the behavior of these unique flying mammals and will help them in future protection measures.
Scientists of the “National Trust” Scotty will study dwarf bats, pypmery batts, as well as brown robes and night batches in Inverevėts in Västero.
Special recorders will be deployed in the main locations, which will track bats activities throughout the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also carry out mobile surveys using manual detectors.
Sound experts will analyze all the records to determine the frequency of basphard cunnings and which species carry out specific activities.
After that, a habitat map and a report will be developed to create a detailed landscape view that reflects bat behavior.
NTS Nature Conservation Adviser Rob Dewar hopes that the results will reveal which Habitats are the most important for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will help to determine the benefits of habitat management works, such as the establishment of meadows, and how best to preserve forests for battens and other associated species.
Over the past centenary, bat population has shrunk significantly in Scotland and the United Kingdom.
These lands are threatened by construction and development works affecting the hiding places of batches and the loss of their place of residence.
Wind turmerators and lighting can also pose a risk, just like munocompairs and chemical treatment products of some building materials, as well as feline attacks.
Actually bats aren't blind.
