The organization has even set up in Scottish the property of Threave in Damfri and Galoway, which hosts eight of ten breeze species found in Scotland.
David Thompson, property manager, argues that this area is perfect for bats.
“Here is a great place for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, a large number of ancient trees and, in general, a suitable habitat.
However, there are still many things that we don’t don't know about bats, so the work we do here and other properties will help us to better understand what they need for survival.”
He emphasizes that it is important to check whether batches do not live in the appropriate place before carrying out property maintenance work, as it can happen that up to 400 females and their pups are killed due to the unintentional destruction of single-women's hiding places, thus possibly destroying the entire local population.
The batches are protected animals and must not be killed, tormented, disturbed or destroyed their hiding places.
Elisabeth Ferrell, an employee of the Bat Protection Fund (Bat Conservation Trust), has urged the public to help.
She said, "We still have a lot to learn about bats, and for a number of species we simply don’t know how they are doing.”
