2004: A powerful earthquake on the west coast of Aceh's northern Sumatra province on December 26 caused a tsunami that affected 14 countries, killing 226 000 people along the coastline of the Indian Ocean, of which more than half were in Aceha.
2005: A series of powerful earthquake straits hit the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people perished on Nia (Nia) on the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake erupted in Java, the most populated islands in Indonesia, in the south, causing a tsunami that crashed into the southern coast, thus killing almost 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude earthquake struck near Padang, the capital of Rietumu Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people died.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude-strong earthquake straddled one of Mentavai's (Mentawai) on the shores of Sumatra, evoking tsunami waves of up to 10 metrics that devastated dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: Earthquake close to the ground surface hit Pidie Jaya's area in Aceh, causing devastation and panic, as it reminded of the exacerbation carried by the devastating embarrassments and tsunami in 2004.
This time tsunami didn't form, but more than 100 people were killed by collaborating buildings.
