Kim management 'decreased" North Korean political asylum seekers
The number of North Korean political asylum seekers in South Korea has declined since Kim Jong came to power seven years ago, the South Korean legislator said.
Park Byeong-seug, citing data from South Korea's unification ministry, said there were 11,27 political asylum seekers last year, compared to 2011, when their numbers amounted to 2706.
The park said a tougher border check-in between North Korea and China and the high human smuggling rates are key factors.
Pyongyang hasn't made public comments.
The vast majority of political asylum seekers from North Korea are eventually offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul says more than 30 000 North Koreans have illegally crossed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most go through China, which has the longest-to-people border with North Korea and is easier to cross it than the heavily guarded Demilitarization Zone (DMZ) between both Koreas.
China considers political asylum seekers as illegal immigrants and deports them forcibly.
Relations between North and South Korea, which are still technically at war, have significantly improved in recent months in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of both countries met Pyongyang to discuss delayed denuclear negotiations.
It happened after a historic meeting in June between US President Donald Trump and Kim Jongun in Singapur, when, on large trains, they agreed to work together to liberate the Korean Peninsula from nuclear weapons.
